message.content:
You are a completeness critic for a plan to merge three Android/Kotlin skill repos (labels: ahmed = everything-claude-code-mobile, chris = chrisbanes/skills, rcosteira = rcosteira79/android-skills) into ONE Claude Code plugin using ahmed's repo as the structural base.

Below are the per-cluster verified findings and merge recommendations from the audit:
[
  {
    "cluster": "compose",
    "recommendation": {
      "cluster": "compose",
      "dominant": "rcosteira",
      "delete": [
        "jetpack-compose"
      ],
      "keepFull": [
        "compose",
        "compose-animations",
        "compose-focus-navigation",
        "compose-modifier-and-layout-style",
        "compose-recomposition-performance",
        "compose-side-effects",
        "compose-slot-api-pattern",
        "compose-stability-diagnostics",
        "compose-state-authoring",
        "compose-state-deferred-reads",
        "compose-state-hoisting",
        "compose-state-holder-ui-split"
      ],
      "partialMerge": [
        {
          "from": "compose-slot-api-pattern",
          "what": "Use chris's correctly-braced default-slot expression `= { SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron() }` to fix rcosteira's non-compiling Rule 3 RIGHT example (CONFIRMED bug B1: `trailingContent: @Composable () -> Unit = SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron()` assigns Unit to a `() -> Unit` param). chris compose-slot-api-pattern already has the correct braced form.",
          "into": "compose"
        },
        {
          "from": "compose",
          "what": "Self-fix (CONFIRMED bug B2, major) in production-crash-playbook.md §5: the keyless `remember { derivedStateOf { allItems.filter { it.name.contains(filter) } } }` reads only plain params and never re-evaluates. Replace with `remember(allItems, filter) { allItems.filter { ... } }` per rcosteira's OWN state-management.md rule. Also correct §5's mislabeled root cause (verifier missedIssue: it is framed as an IndexOutOfBounds count desync but the dominant defect is that the derivedStateOf reads no Compose State).",
          "into": "compose"
        },
        {
          "from": "compose",
          "what": "Self-fix (CONFIRMED bug B3, minor) in performance.md GOOD example: replace `remember(items, query) { derivedStateOf { items.filter { query in it.title } } }` with `remember(items, query) { items.filter { ... } }` (derivedStateOf over plain keyed values adds pure overhead), and DELETE the false comment 'recomposes only when the filtered result actually changes' (verifier missedIssue: keying remember recreates the State each input change, so the advertised dedup is unachievable as written). Contradicts rcosteira's own state-management.md line 60.",
          "into": "compose"
        },
        {
          "from": "compose",
          "what": "Self-fix (CONFIRMED bug B4, minor) in animation-recipes.md shimmer: replace `fun Modifier.shimmerEffect() = composed { ... }` with the `Modifier.Node`/`ModifierNodeElement` API, per rcosteira's own modifiers.md line 203 and deprecated-patterns.md line 18 which mark `composed {}` deprecated for new custom modifiers.",
          "into": "compose"
        },
        {
          "from": "compose",
          "what": "Self-fix (CONFIRMED bug B5, minor) in production-crash-playbook.md §7 SafeShimmerItem: remove the `runCatching { Canvas(...) { drawRect(brush) } }` + `result.isFailure` fallback (dead code — the draw lambda runs in the draw phase after composition returns, so a Skia draw crash cannot propagate into runCatching). Keep only the real guard `if (size.minDimension <= 0f) return@Canvas`.",
          "into": "compose"
        },
        {
          "from": "compose",
          "what": "Self-fix (CONFIRMED bug B6, minor) in production-crash-playbook.md §4 dedup-index: `withDedupIndex()` returns `item.also { it.dedupIndex = count }`, mutating caller-owned instances in place; on same-instance duplicates (e.g. list + list) both entries end up keyed `id_1` and the duplicate-key crash still fires. Make it a pure copy (data-class copy, not in-place `also{}`) and dedup by list position, not by mutating shared objects.",
          "into": "compose"
        }
      ],
      "manualResolution": [
        "C1 (CONFIRMED contradiction) — how to type an optional @Composable slot. Three live positions: (a) ahmed empty-lambda `@Composable () -> Unit = {}` (being deleted, but note: this exactly matches the real androidx Material 3 TopAppBar signature, so it is a genuine framework-convention stance, not an error); (b) chris nullable `(@Composable () -> Unit)? = null` so the component can omit the slot's container/spacing when absent (matches Material 3 ListItem); (c) rcosteira view-composition Rule 3 keeps a NON-nullable composable default EXPRESSION (`= SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron()`) so a default renders automatically. Human must pick one house convention and apply it across the kept `compose` and `compose-slot-api-pattern` skills, which currently give opposing advice. (Verifier missedIssue: chris-vs-rcosteira also diverge on nullable-null vs non-nullable-default-expression, an axis the finder did not separately flag.)",
        "C2 (CONFIRMED contradiction) — may a reusable composable hardcode a default intrinsic size on its root modifier? chris (compose-modifier-and-layout-style) says YES for a component's identity size, e.g. Avatar = `modifier.clip(CircleShape).size(48.dp)`, placed AFTER the caller's modifier so a caller `.size(...)` still wins. rcosteira (modifiers.md Rule 4) says NO: the root MUST NOT carry any sizing/placement decision; its identity carve-out names ONLY `.clip(CircleShape)`, and the RIGHT Avatar is `modifier.clip(CircleShape)` with no size. Both agree caller-first ordering wins; the genuine either/or is whether a default size may live on the root at all. Human must decide the house rule and reconcile both kept skills."
      ],
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    "confirmedContradictions": [
      {
        "topic": "Optional @Composable slot default: empty-lambda vs nullable",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Re-read all three sources. ahmed jetpack-compose/SKILL.md lines 68-69 literally declare `navigationIcon: @Composable () -> Unit = {}` and `actions: @Composable RowScope.() -> Unit = {}` (empty-lambda defaults). chris compose-slot-api-pattern/SKILL.md §3 lines 112-122 explicitly state 'prefer `(@Composable () -> Unit)? = null` over `@Composable () -> Unit = {}`' and its quick-reference row 165 flags the empty-lambda default as a smell to fix. rcosteira references/view-composition.md Rule 2 lines 37-41 says the same ('DON'T use `slot: @Composable () -> Unit = {}`... an empty lambda is still invoked... producing ghost space'). These are directly opposing prescriptions for the same design decision, not different depth/wording. Nuance worth recording: ahmed's empty-lambda choice actually matches the real Material 3 TopAppBar signature (navigationIcon/actions default to `{}`), so this is a genuine philosophical disagreement between authors rather than a simple ahmed error — but the advice still conflicts. CONFIRMED."
      },
      {
        "topic": "May a reusable composable hardcode a default size on its root modifier?",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "chris compose-modifier-and-layout-style/SKILL.md §2 line 111 shows the GOOD Avatar body as `modifier.clip(CircleShape).size(48.dp)`, and §3 line 139 explicitly calls the default `.size(48.dp)` part of what 'makes an Avatar an avatar' (identity carve-out, placed after the caller's modifier so a caller `.size(...)` still wins per line 116). rcosteira references/modifiers.md takes the opposite line: Rule 3 line 83 uses a hardcoded `.size(48.dp)` as the very example of a modifier that wrongly overrides a caller, Rule 4 line 87 says the root 'MUST NOT carry `.fillMaxWidth()`, `.height(56.dp)`... or any other placement decision', and its RIGHT Avatar (lines 78-80) is `Box(modifier = modifier.clip(CircleShape))` with no size. Its identity carve-out (lines 67, 115) names ONLY `.clip(CircleShape)`, never size. So a reviewer following chris keeps `.size(48.dp)` on the root; one following rcosteira flags it. Both agree on the ordering mechanics (caller-first size wins), so the conflict is narrowly but genuinely about whether a default intrinsic size may live on the root at all. CONFIRMED."
      }
    ],
    "confirmedBugs": [
      {
        "description": "rcosteira view-composition.md Rule 3 RIGHT example `trailingContent: @Composable () -> Unit = SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron()` does not compile (needs braces).",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Verified lines 85 and 93-96. `object SettingsRowDefaults { @Composable fun Chevron() = Icon(...) }` — `Chevron()` returns Unit (Icon returns Unit). The default `= SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron()` is a call, so it evaluates to Unit and is assigned to a parameter typed `@Composable () -> Unit`: 'Type mismatch: inferred type is Unit but @Composable () -> Unit was expected.' The correct form is `= { SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron() }`. chris compose-slot-api-pattern/SKILL.md line 150 wraps the identical call correctly with braces. This is the central RIGHT example of Rule 3 in a skill that teaches correct APIs, so a non-compiling example is a real, material defect. CONFIRMED."
      },
      {
        "description": "rcosteira production-crash-playbook.md §5 Fix introduces a stale-state bug: keyless `remember { derivedStateOf { allItems.filter { it.name.contains(filter) } } }` reads only plain params, so it never updates.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Verified lines 288-291. `allItems` and `filter` are plain function parameters (signature line 288). `remember { }` has no keys, so it runs once and caches the derivedStateOf created at first composition. `derivedStateOf` only re-evaluates when a Compose `State` read inside its lambda changes — here it reads no State, only the once-captured plain values. So when a new `filter`/`allItems` is passed, FilteredList recomposes but `filteredItems` returns the original filtered list forever. This is a genuine, user-visible correctness bug (the filter appears broken) and it directly violates the same plugin's state-management.md lines 50-60 ('captured once... silently uses the original forever' / 'the block must read at least one Compose State to ever re-evaluate'). Correct form: `remember(allItems, filter) { allItems.filter { ... } }`. CONFIRMED."
      },
      {
        "description": "rcosteira performance.md GOOD example `remember(items, query) { derivedStateOf { items.filter { query in it.title } } }` wraps plain values in derivedStateOf for no benefit.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Verified lines 258-265. The BAD line 260 treats `items`/`query` as plain in-scope values; the GOOD line 263 reads those same plain values inside `derivedStateOf` while keying the surrounding `remember` on them. Because the remember is keyed on (items, query), the derivedStateOf is recreated on every input change, producing a fresh State each time — so the advertised dedup ('recomposes only when the filtered result actually changes', line 262) is NOT delivered, and the derivedStateOf reads no Compose State so it adds pure overhead. This exactly contradicts the same plugin's state-management.md line 60 ('derivedStateOf { a + b } over two plain values pays overhead for nothing; use remember { a + b }'). Correct: `remember(items, query) { items.filter { ... } }`. As the finding states, it is not a correctness bug (keyed remember keeps it fresh) but a real, minor internal inconsistency — and the GOOD comment is itself misleading. CONFIRMED."
      },
      {
        "description": "rcosteira animation-recipes.md shimmer uses `composed { }`, which the same plugin marks deprecated.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Verified animation-recipes.md line 10: `fun Modifier.shimmerEffect(): Modifier = composed { ... }`. modifiers.md line 203 says 'prefer the `Modifier.Node` / `ModifierNodeElement` API over the deprecated `composed { }`' and deprecated-patterns.md line 18 lists '`Modifier.composed { }` → `Modifier.Node` / `ModifierNodeElement`... Prefer Node for new custom modifiers'. The shimmer recipe authors a NEW custom modifier, so per the plugin's own rule it should use Node. Genuine internal inconsistency. Correctly scoped as minor by the finding (composed{} still functions, and deprecated-patterns line 18 hedges 'benchmark before migrating existing ones'), but for a newly-taught modifier the guidance is self-contradictory. CONFIRMED."
      },
      {
        "description": "rcosteira production-crash-playbook.md §7 SafeShimmerItem wraps a composable Canvas in runCatching, which cannot catch the draw-phase crash it targets.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Verified lines 410-423. `Canvas(modifier) { ... drawRect(brush) }` is `Spacer(modifier.drawBehind(onDraw))`; the onDraw lambda executes in the draw phase, on a later render traversal, after the composable `Canvas(...)` call (and thus the surrounding `runCatching`) has already returned during composition. A Skia draw-time exception therefore cannot propagate into the `runCatching`, so `result.isFailure` is never true for the targeted crash and the fallback Box (lines 416-423) is dead code for that path. Only the explicit `if (size.minDimension <= 0f) return@Canvas` guard (line 412) provides real protection. Real defect giving a false sense of safety; minor severity is appropriate. CONFIRMED."
      },
      {
        "description": "rcosteira production-crash-playbook.md §4 dedup-index mutates caller-owned data and collides on same-instance duplicates.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Verified lines 212-243. `Notification` has a mutable `var dedupIndex` outside the constructor, and `withDedupIndex()` returns `item.also { it.dedupIndex = count }` — a purported pure transform that mutates the caller's original instances in place (the returned list holds the same references as the input). Two real problems: (1) side-effecting caller-owned objects is a genuine defect for a transformation function; (2) if the SAME instance appears twice (e.g. `list + list`, reference-overlapping concatenation on reconnect), both iterations write to one object — first sets dedupIndex=0, second sets 1 — leaving both list entries pointing at the object with dedupIndex=1, so both keys become `id_1` and the duplicate-key crash still fires, falsifying the 'Guaranteed unique' comment (line 237). The pattern does work for the more common case (distinct instances sharing an id, e.g. re-deserialized WebSocket messages), so the hole is on the same-instance edge, but the mutation smell is unconditional. CONFIRMED."
      }
    ],
    "missed": [
      "chris vs rcosteira disagree on a related axis the finder did not flag: how an optional-but-defaulted slot should be typed. chris compose-slot-api-pattern §1/§3 (lines 47-48, 118-119) makes it nullable `(@Composable () -> Unit)? = null` (matching Material 3 ListItem, no auto-default), while rcosteira view-composition Rule 3 line 85 keeps a non-nullable composable default expression `= SettingsRowDefaults.Chevron()` on the parameter so a default renders automatically. Both cite Material 3. This is a genuine divergence adjacent to contradiction C1 that was not surfaced.",
      "performance.md §derivedStateOf: beyond the overhead point in bug B3, the GOOD example's comment 'recomposes only when the filtered result actually changes' (line 262) is itself false — keying `remember(items, query)` recreates the derivedStateOf on every input change, yielding a new State object each time and forcing a downstream recompose regardless of whether the result changed, so the advertised dedup is defeated. The finding noted the overhead but not that the stated benefit is unachievable as written.",
      "production-crash-playbook §5 mislabels its own root cause: the section frames the BAD example (lines 269-278) as a cross-frame count/collection desync (IndexOutOfBounds), but the dominant defect in BOTH the BAD and the Fix is that the keyless `remember { derivedStateOf { ... } }` over plain params never re-evaluates at all (same as bug B2). The 'derivedStateOf recalculates lazily when its inputs change' premise (line 260) is wrong here because the inputs are not Compose State.",
      "Context that strengthens contradiction C1 (not itself a bug): ahmed's empty-lambda slot defaults (`= {}`) exactly match the real androidx Material 3 TopAppBar signature, so ahmed is following the framework's own convention rather than making an error — the conflict with chris/rcosteira is a real design disagreement, which the finding could have noted to avoid implying ahmed is simply outdated."
    ]
  },
  {
    "cluster": "coroutines-flow",
    "recommendation": {
      "cluster": "coroutines-flow",
      "dominant": "complementary",
      "delete": [
        "coroutines-patterns",
        "shared-coroutines"
      ],
      "keepFull": [
        "kotlin-coroutines-structured-concurrency",
        "kotlin-flow-state-event-modeling",
        "kotlin-coroutines",
        "kotlin-flows"
      ],
      "partialMerge": [
        {
          "from": "shared-coroutines",
          "what": "Salvage ONLY the clean, unique, correct KMP scaffolding before deleting the folder: (1) the expect/actual PlatformDispatcher abstraction including the real iOS GCD-backed dispatchers (NSQueueDispatcher / DispatchQueueDispatcher over dispatch_get_main_queue / global queues); (2) the build.gradle.kts commonMain/androidMain sourceSets setup for kotlinx-coroutines; (3) the BaseRepository / BaseUseCase / BaseNoParamsUseCase / FlowUseCase base classes, which correctly wrap results in Result and DO rethrow CancellationException; (4) the TestPlatformDispatcher test helper. Do NOT carry over the parts that die with the folder (all verifier-CONFIRMED bugs or contradicted advice): networkCall (withTimeout for network timeouts — contradicted by rcosteira; OkHttp/Retrofit/Ktor own those), BackgroundWorker (swallows CancellationException, logs false 'failed'), cachedIn (delegates to undefined `cached` symbol — will not compile), shareIn (no-op that returns the receiver and shadows the real operator), collectWhileActive (never-removed lifecycle observer + never-cancelled job leak, no ON_DESTROY), repeatOnViewActive (early completion + duplicate/stale emits + null-sentinel drop), flowFrom/observeUser (one-shot cold flow mislabeled as an observe stream), and useCaseScope() (mints an uncancellable leaking scope per call).",
          "into": "kotlin-coroutines"
        }
      ],
      "manualResolution": [
        "Two overlapping coroutines skills: keep chris's kotlin-coroutines-structured-concurrency (review-oriented anti-pattern playbook with ranked refactors, DI-singleton/Initializer §5, ContentProvider runBlocking carve-out, red-flag tables) AND rcosteira's kotlin-coroutines (terse modern rules: main-safety ownership rule, suspendRunCatching, viewModelScope-already-SupervisorJob, MainScope warning) as two complementary skills, OR merge them into one canonical coroutines skill. They agree on every correctness point but overlap substantially; both hold unique value, so this is an editorial call, not a correctness one.",
        "Two Flow skills: chris's kotlin-flow-state-event-modeling covers state/event PRIMITIVE selection (StateFlow/SharedFlow/Channel choice, stateIn-in-function trap, .map-on-StateFlow, update{} atomicity, phasing) while rcosteira's kotlin-flows covers OPERATOR traps and callback bridging (callbackFlow/awaitClose, retryWhen attempt guard, combine 'stuck on Loading', Kotlin 2.4 backing fields, tryEmit drop semantics). Decide whether to keep them as two focused skills or merge into one comprehensive Flow skill.",
        "Home for the salvaged ahmed KMP scaffolding: fold it into rcosteira's kotlin-coroutines (which already gives KMP dispatcher/scope injection guidance, as recommended in partialMerge) OR give it its own dedicated KMP-coroutines-scaffolding skill. Before either, audit the existing kmp-* skills in the android-skills plugin (kmp-multiplatform, kmp-interop, etc.) so the expect/actual dispatcher content is not duplicated.",
        "ApplicationScope/ViewScope (ahmed shared-coroutines) each expose cancel(), which partly satisfies chris's carve-out that application-scoped owners may hold a scope IF they expose explicit cancel/close. Decide whether to keep them as in-class SupervisorJob-owning scope holders or adopt chris's preferred pattern of injecting a single Application.applicationScope. This is a legitimate design either/or — distinct from the useCaseScope() factory, which is a decided bug and dropped."
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    "confirmedContradictions": [
      {
        "topic": "Primitive for one-shot UI events (navigation / snackbars)",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "ahmed coroutines-patterns/SKILL.md lines 63-65 present a default `MutableSharedFlow<Event>()` (zero replay, zero buffer) exposed via asSharedFlow() as the 'SharedFlow for events' pattern, inside a ViewModel+Compose context. chris kotlin-flow-state-event-modeling/SKILL.md lines 27-37 mark the byte-identical pattern `// BAD` and recommend `Channel(Channel.BUFFERED).receiveAsFlow()` `// GOOD`, with line 26 stating verbatim 'If nothing is collecting at the exact instant of emission, the event is gone.' rcosteira kotlin-flows/SKILL.md line 16 marks Channel(BUFFERED).receiveAsFlow() 'Keep - correct' for single-consumer fire-once events and line 22 states 'SharedFlow(replay = 0) drops the emission if no collector is active.' This is genuine opposing advice for the dominant use case. Nuance: all three agree SharedFlow IS correct for true multi-observer broadcast (chris decision table line 152), and ahmed's label 'events' doesn't specify consumer count -- but a zero-buffer default MutableSharedFlow for UI events is exactly the lossy configuration the other two flag as a bug."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Catching broad Exception around a suspend call in a coroutine",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "ahmed coroutines-patterns/SKILL.md lines 90-97 present the primary 'Error Handling' example: viewModelScope.launch { try { repository.fetchData(); _state.value=Success } catch (e: Exception) { _state.value=Error } } -- a broad catch around a suspend call with no CancellationException rethrow (CancellationException extends Exception, so it is caught). rcosteira kotlin-coroutines/SKILL.md line 57 says 'catch (e: Exception) / Throwable swallows CancellationException and breaks structured cancellation', line 60 'never Exception / Throwable', line 61 'Never catch CancellationException without rethrowing.' chris §6 lines 281-289 mark the identical shape (catch Exception around api.load()) 'BAD - catches CancellationException, never rethrows.' Real opposing advice. Reinforced by the fact that ahmed's own Cancellation section (lines 117-125) shows the correct `catch (e: CancellationException) { throw e }` guard, so ahmed's recommended example contradicts even its own file."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Manufacturing/owning a CoroutineScope in a helper factory, and using CoroutineExceptionHandler as the remedy",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "ahmed shared-coroutines/SKILL.md lines 122-129 define `fun useCaseScope(): CoroutineScope = CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob()+PlatformDispatchers.io+CoroutineExceptionHandler{...})` as a reusable pattern that mints a fresh, lifecycle-unbound scope on every call; lines 94-106/109-119 build ApplicationScope/ViewScope whose safety rests on an attached CoroutineExceptionHandler. chris kotlin-coroutines-structured-concurrency/SKILL.md line 145 states a scope instantiated in a function body 'is just a stored scope with extra steps. Each call leaks a new uncancellable scope ... the scope shouldn't exist at all', and the red-flags table line 430 answers 'I''ll just add a CoroutineExceptionHandler to the scope' with 'The problem isn''t error handling. The problem is the scope shouldn''t exist.' The useCaseScope factory and the CEH-as-remedy are directly contradicted. Partial nuance: chris's carve-out (line 420) does permit application-scoped classes to own a scope when they expose explicit cancel/close -- ahmed's ApplicationScope (cancel(), lines 103-105) and ViewScope (cancel(), lines 115-118) partly satisfy that, though chris prefers injecting Application.applicationScope over constructing SupervisorJob in-class. The core opposition (helper-minted scopes + CEH as the fix) is real."
      },
      {
        "topic": "Using withTimeout for network-call timeouts",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "ahmed shared-coroutines/SKILL.md lines 249-256 provide `protected suspend fun <T> networkCall(timeout: Duration = 30.seconds, block) = withTimeout(timeout) { withContext(PlatformDispatchers.io) { block() } }` as the standard network wrapper. rcosteira kotlin-coroutines/SKILL.md line 49 states verbatim '(Don''t reach for withTimeout for network timeouts -- OkHttp/Retrofit/Ktor own those.)' ahmed builds a reusable helper that does exactly what rcosteira says not to reach for, for the same purpose (network calls). Direct opposing advice."
      }
    ],
    "confirmedBugs": [
      {
        "description": "ahmed coroutines-patterns primary ViewModel error-handling catches broad Exception around a suspend call without rethrowing CancellationException.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Lines 90-97 verified exactly as quoted. CancellationException (kotlin.coroutines.cancellation.CancellationException; on JVM extends IllegalStateException->RuntimeException->Exception) is caught by `catch (e: Exception)`, so when the coroutine is cancelled during the suspend `repository.fetchData()`, the cancellation is swallowed and `_state.value = Error(e.message)` runs instead of propagating cancellation -- breaking the structured-concurrency contract. This is a well-recognized anti-pattern, explicitly forbidden by both chris (§6) and rcosteira (lines 57-61), and it directly contradicts the same file's own Cancellation section (lines 117-125). Real defect in taught guidance; 'major' severity is justified for a copy-paste cheat-sheet."
      },
      {
        "description": "ahmed shared-coroutines BackgroundWorker periodic loop catches broad Exception around work() inside while(isActive), swallowing CancellationException and logging a false 'failed'.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Lines 365-374 verified. work() is a suspend call; if the scope is cancelled while it is suspended, the thrown CancellationException is caught by `catch (e: Exception)`, logged as 'Worker $key failed', and execution continues. It is a genuine instance of the swallowed-cancellation anti-pattern forbidden by chris §6 and rcosteira lines 57-61. The finding accurately notes that the following `delay(interval)` (outside the try) does eventually re-throw and propagate the cancel, so the loop is not permanently uncancellable -- the real harm is a spurious 'failed' log plus one extra iteration window. Bug is real; 'major' severity is arguably overstated (delay rescues cancellation), but the defect and its contradiction of the other sources are confirmed."
      },
      {
        "description": "ahmed shared-coroutines Flow<T>.cachedIn delegates to a free function cached(scope) that does not exist.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Lines 147-149 verified: `fun <T> Flow<T>.cachedIn(scope): Flow<T> = cached(scope)`. No `cached` function or extension exists in kotlinx.coroutines or the Kotlin stdlib; AndroidX Paging exposes `Flow<PagingData<T>>.cachedIn(scope)` (which is also unavailable in a KMP commonMain source set). The file defines no local `cached` symbol. The reference is undefined -- will not compile. Real bug."
      },
      {
        "description": "ahmed shared-coroutines custom StateFlow<T>.shareIn ignores all parameters and returns the receiver unchanged.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Lines 138-144 verified: the function takes scope/started/replay and simply `return this`. It is a misleading no-op -- a caller writing myStateFlow.shareIn(scope, started, replay) believes they configured sharing/replay but gets the original flow untouched. It also collides conceptually with the real kotlinx.coroutines operator (`Flow<T>.shareIn(...): SharedFlow<T>`); because this overload is defined on StateFlow with a StateFlow return, for a StateFlow receiver it would be preferred and silently do nothing. Real footgun; 'minor' severity fair."
      },
      {
        "description": "ahmed shared-coroutines collectWhileActive leaks the lifecycle observer, never cancels the returned SupervisorJob, and lacks an ON_DESTROY branch.",
        "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
        "reasoning": "Lines 156-181 verified. The `lifecycle.subscribe { ... }` observer is registered and never removed (no unsubscribe/removeObserver), so the closure -- capturing `scope`/`job` -- outlives the lifecycle. Only ON_RESUME (launches collect) and ON_PAUSE (job.cancelChildren()) are handled; there is no ON_DESTROY, and the returned SupervisorJob `job` is never cancelled by the helper, so the scope survives destruction. Additionally it depends on a non-standard `Lifecycle.subscribe` API (AndroidX Lifecycle uses addObserver with a LifecycleEventObserver, not subscribe). Genuine resource leak; 'minor' fair."
      },
      {
